"""
robust_csv_parse — parse dirty CSV with detection, not guessing.

WHAT IT DOES: Real-world CSV is dirty: quotes break, fields span lines, exports
truncate. This parses a CSV line (or file) with detection instead of guessing:
strict mode raises a precise error on malformed quoting; non-strict mode skips broken
rows so one bad line doesn't kill a 10k-row import. Uses Python's stdlib csv module
with strict=True so malformed quoting is actually detected.

WHEN TO USE: You're ingesting CSV from an uncontrolled source (user uploads,
third-party exports, legacy systems) and need to distinguish broken from foreign.
The parser handles any language/symbols in fields — Ukrainian, CJK, € — all parse
cleanly.

GUARANTEES: Strict mode raises ValueError with "Malformed CSV: <reason>" on genuinely
malformed rows (unclosed quotes, data after closing quote). Non-strict mode returns
None for malformed rows. Empty input returns [] (no row). Quoted fields preserve
embedded commas and newlines. Non-ASCII data parses cleanly.

NOT: A general CSV parser. Uses the stdlib csv module under the hood — no custom
state machine. None as input is silently coerced to [] (AURA-E006).
"""
